(project borge-orchestration-deprecation
 (doc "Retiring borge's orchestration half: the pi harness, phase lock, merge queue, and inbox. Plan, not a nuke.")
 (status planned)
 (no-inline
 )
 (section thesis
  (doc "What is being deprecated and what is not")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Borge has two halves that grew together and should not survive
        together.

        KEPT (the gates):
          borge balance, parse, check, fmt, drift, lint, report, book,
          inline, nodes, stats, log, diff, normalize, version, modules,
          print.

          These are the parts that make borge borge: a spec format, a
          structural checker, a drift detector, and a renderer. They are
          deterministic, they compare files to files, and they run in a
          git hook or CI without asking anyone's opinion.

        DEPRECATED (the orchestration):
          plan, make, execute, generate, review (agent mode), commit,
          abort, merge, merge-queue, inbox, issue.

          These all presuppose that borge is the thing driving an agent:
          it writes a prompt, launches pi, holds a lock, watches an exit
          code, commits on success, and mediates conflicts between
          concurrent agent branches. That is a harness, and it is the
          failed experiment: the loop is duplicated three times
          (bin/cmd/Plan.ml, Make.ml, Drift.ml run_agent), the lock is a
          snapshot mechanism pretending to be isolation, and the merge
          queue solves a concurrency problem borge created by launching
          agents itself.

        The boundary test for anything in between: does it require
        borge to know what an agent is? If yes, it is orchestration. If
        it only needs an editor and git, it stays.

        The replacement is not "no workflow". It is that the agent
        runtime (rein, or pi invoked by a human, or an editor) owns the
        loop, the lock, and the merge, and borge owns the gates it can
        actually enforce: balance before edit, drift after edit, lint
        before commit. Borge becomes a thing you run, not a thing that
        runs.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section inventory
  (doc "What actually gets deleted, and how much code that is")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Measured on 2026-10-26 from the repo, not from memory:

        ORCHESTRATION LIBRARIES (delete):
          lib/lock/         lock.ml 228, lock_prompt.ml 62,
                            lock_pi_launch.ml 7, prompt_minimal.ml 140  = 437
          lib/merge/        agent_submit 225, branch 187,
                            conflict_detect 150, lockfile 166,
                            merge_strategies 184, queue_storage 139,
                            queue_types 162, rollback 162,
                            worktree 163                              = 1538
          lib/inbox/        inbox.ml 391
          lib/distributed/  borg_comment 121, module_spec 122            = 243
          lib/bug/          bug_ast 82, bug_parse 82,
                            bug_registry 49, bug_write 41
                            (already superseded by inbox per
                            lib/inbox/inbox.borg)                          = 254
          lib/generate/     agent.ml 291 (pi print-mode invocation),
                            generate.ml 183 (spec/code generation),
                            review_quality.ml 115                            = 589
          lib/review/       review_agent 133, review_prompt 179,
                            review_parse 221, semantic_review 351,
                            review_types 144, extract_docs 164,
                            extract_functions 125, extract_parsetree 57
                            (all LLM-facing)                              = 1374

        ORCHESTRATION COMMANDS (delete):
          Plan 115, Make 115, Execute 40, Generate 323, Review 205,
          Commit 82, Abort 43, Undo 41, Merge 163, Merge_queue 275,
          Inbox 174, Issue 131                                 = 1707

        Roughly 6,350 of the repo's 19,638 lines of ML (lib/ and bin/
        together). Not a small edit, which is exactly why it is a plan
        and not tonight's commit.

        ALSO AFFECTED:
          bin/borge.ml        — command list and man page
          bin/cmd/Drift.ml    — run_agent (~70 lines) uses Lock + Lock_pi_launch
          bin/cmd/Modules.ml  — uses distributed/module_spec
          lib/config.ml       — api-keys block, agent config, exists only
                                for the LLM half

        PI COUPLING (the whole footprint):
          lib/lock/lock_pi_launch.ml:4   `pi @prompt`
          lib/generate/agent.ml:138      `command -v pi`, then
          lib/generate/agent.ml:172      `pi -p --no-session --no-tools`
          plus BORGE_PI_PATH and BORGE_AGENT_CMD environment variables.
          Four sites. The harness is not deeply woven in; it is worn on
          top, which is why removing it is tractable.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section keep-set
  (doc "What survives and what it has to learn")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Survivors and their dependencies on the deprecated half:

          balance, parse, fmt, normalize, nodes, print, inline, version
                        — no dependencies. Untouched.
          check         — uses Check.run only for --worktree's dune
                          build step, which becomes conv.build
                          (see convention-abstract §gates).
          lint          — check_comment_integrity shells `git diff HEAD`,
                          which is fine and stays. check_literacy and
                          check_code_doc are --code-doc/--literacy opt-in
                          and stay.
          drift         — static path unchanged once the convention
                          record lands; run_agent is deleted.
          report        — survives whole. This is the dashboard and it
                          never needed an agent.
          book          — survives whole (print/export/manifest).
          stats         — survives; needs conv.component_ext.
          modules       — currently reads distributed/module_spec. Either
                          reimplement on Spec.extract_implements (the
                          data is in the .borg (implements) forms) or
                          delete with the rest. Leaning reimplement: it
                          is 73 lines of CLI over spec data.
          log, diff     — survive. `log` is git log over .borg files,
                          `diff` is git diff with status annotations.
                          Neither needs a lock.

        The point of listing this: the gate set is self-contained. Every
        survivor's dependency on the deprecated half is either none or a
        single line that has a named replacement.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section phases
  (doc "Five phases, each independently revertable")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Phase A — freeze and announce (no code).
          1. Mark every deprecated command's .borg section
             (status deprecated) with a doc body naming the replacement.
             borge report already counts deprecated; this makes the
             count visible and honest instead of a backlog nobody reads.
          2. Add a runtime deprecation banner: one stderr line on each
             deprecated command naming the phase and the date. Exit code
             unchanged. This is the cheapest thing that stops new usage
             from accreting.
          3. Write down the keep-set boundary (§thesis) in AGENTS.md so
             new features get filed against the right half.

          Gate: no new orchestration code lands after this phase. Any
          PR touching lib/lock, lib/merge, lib/inbox, lib/generate, or
          lib/review is a bug fix only.

        Phase B — cut the pi dependency (smallest reversible step).
          Delete lib/lock/lock_pi_launch.ml and the pi branch of
          Agent.run_pi_print. Deprecated commands then fail with
          "orchestration removed; run your agent yourself" instead of
          launching pi. Everything else keeps working — the lock snapshot
          and merge queue are still there, just not useful.
          Why first: it severs the harness on 7 lines, and it is the
          change that makes the rest obviously dead rather than
          plausibly-needed.

        Phase C — kill the merge machinery.
          Delete lib/merge (1538 lines) and the merge, merge-queue, and
          agent-submit commands. Nothing in the keep-set imports them.
          Verify: dune build, then `borge check --worktree` and
          `borge drift` on three OCaml and three Go repos.
          Also delete lib/distributed and reimplement `borge modules` on
          Spec.extract_implements in the same commit, so the command
          does not blink out and back.

        Phase D — kill the loops and the lock.
          Delete plan, make, execute, generate, review, commit, abort,
          lib/lock (437), lib/generate, lib/review, and
          Drift.run_agent. This is the big one and the least risky,
          because by now nothing depends on it: C removed the merge
          queue's consumers, B removed pi, and C removed the shared
          lockfile types.

          `borge undo` SURVIVES and was wrongly listed here when this
          chapter was first written. Read at bin/cmd/Undo.ml, it is a
          `git revert HEAD` wrapper with output — no lock, no prompt, no
          subprocess that talks to a model. It fails the §thesis
          boundary test (it does not need to know what an agent is) and
          it is the only convenience wrapper over git that the gate
          half benefits from directly after a bad commit. Keeping it.

        Phase E — kill the inbox.
          Delete lib/inbox, lib/bug, and the inbox/issue commands.
          Inbox exists to give a gated agent somewhere to file scope
          concerns instead of editing the spec. With no gated agent, the
          queue has no producer.
          Before deleting: check for live .borge-inbox files in other
          repos and convert them (they are plain sexp; a one-shot
          markdown dump is enough). Same for lib/bug.

        Ordering rationale: B severs the harness, C removes the
        concurrency machinery that only the harness needed, D removes
        the loops that only the harness ran, E removes the queue that
        only the gated harness wrote to. Each phase leaves the tree
        building and linting.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section migration
  (doc "Where the behavior goes, so it is not just deleted")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Deprecating is not the same as dropping. Each removed
        capability has a named home, and if it does not, that is a
        signal it was never real:

          phase gating (plan vs make)     -> the agent runtime's own
                                            tool permissions. Rein already
                                            has Rein_gate.Borge_rules,
                                            and it is the thing holding
                                            the editor, so it is the
                                            thing that can refuse a write.
          transactional isolation         -> git worktrees, created by the
                                            human or the runtime, not by
                                            borge.
          merge queue / conflict handling -> ordinary git. The queue only
                                            existed because borge launched
                                            N agents into one tree.
          prompt assembly                 -> whatever prompt the runtime
                                            uses. It does not need
                                            borge's role enum.
          LLM semantic review             -> keep as a manual tool only if
                                            wanted: `borge review` becomes
                                            a thin wrapper that reads
                                            files and prints a prompt to
                                            stdout for a human to paste.
                                            No subprocess, no temp files,
                                            no parsing of responses.
                                            Consider deleting entirely.
          inbox filing                    -> a plain markdown TODO file, or
                                            the runtime's own queue.

        Note on rein: rein's lib/rein_borge has borge_tool (runs balance,
        check, drift, fmt), borge_gate (file protection), borge_inbox
        (files inbox items), and borge_subagent (specs for "borge make/
        drift/check"). borge_tool and borge_gate are exactly the keep-set
        and should be unaffected. borge_subagent and borge_inbox die with
        Phases D and E, and rein's dune.disabled in lib/rein_borge
        already shows the module is not currently wired — so this is a
        cleanup, not a break.

        Also live to fix: ~/dev/wyo.tech/rein/.borge.lock/ has been
        sitting in state "running" since a session that ended without
        teardown. After Phase D nothing will ever clean it up, so it
        should be removed and the whole snapshot mechanism documented as
        gone.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section risks
  (doc "What could go wrong, and the check for each")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        1. Deleting something the keep-set actually needs. Check:
           after each phase, `dune build @all` plus a grep for the
           module name outside its own directory. The keep-set
           dependency audit in §keep-set is the source list.

        2. Losing spec prose that only lives in an orchestration
           chapter. docs/agent.borg is 845 lines and mostly about plan/
           make/execute. Its durable content — comment discipline, the
           case for gating, the "spec as truth" argument — belongs in
           the root book and docs/literate.borg. Rewrite before delete;
           do not let the book lose its argument along with its code.

        3. Silently breaking `borge modules` or `report` by removing a
           shared type. Check: run all keep-set commands against borge
           itself and one Go repo after each phase, diff the output
           against the previous phase.

        4. The trap of replacing orchestration with orchestration. The
           deprecation is only complete if borge stops knowing what an
           agent is. A new `borge run` that shells out to rein would
           recreate the problem with a new name. If a phase starts
           growing a subprocess that talks to a model, stop.

        5. Version/name churn. The CLI loses 12 of 28 commands. Worth a
           major version bump and a README rewrite that describes borge
           as a spec toolkit, not "a spec-driven code orchestrator" —
           the tagline in bin/borge.ml and borge.opam is itself the
           deprecated claim.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section done
  (doc "How we know it is finished")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        1. bin/borge.ml lists no command that launches, prompts, or
           locks. Its man page's WORKFLOW section describes edit ->
           balance -> fmt -> check -> report, with no agent.
        2. `grep -ri "pi \|pi -p\|BORGE_PI_PATH\|BORGE_AGENT_CMD" lib bin`
           returns nothing.
        3. `grep -rn "borge.lock" lib bin` returns nothing, and the
           .borge.lock/ directory is gone from every local repo.
        4. borge.opam and the CLI description no longer say
           "orchestrator".
        5. The gate commands (balance, fmt, check, drift, lint, report,
           book, stats, inline, nodes, log, diff, parse, normalize,
           modules, print, version) all run on borge, on three OCaml
           repos, and on three Go repos, with drift output identical to
           the pre-deprecation baseline captured in Phase A.
        6. Adding convention number three still touches no file outside
           the convention module — proving the two efforts compose and
           that borge's remaining surface is the part that scales to
           languages.
      |))
  )
 )
)
